---
import SiteLayout from '../../components/SiteLayout.astro';
import TechnicalFlow from '../../components/TechnicalFlow.astro';
---
<SiteLayout title="About Ember — a clearer Monero mining manager" description="What Ember is building for Windows Monero mining, how its current foundation works, and what remains in development.">
  <div class="wrap">
    <header class="page-intro product-intro"><div><span class="page-kicker">EMBER / THE PRODUCT</span><h1>More of the mining process, made understandable.</h1><p>Ember is a Windows desktop project that helps people configure and manage Monero CPU mining without hiding the important choices.</p></div><p class="release-note">IN DEVELOPMENT<br />There is no public release or download yet. Production mining verification and release hardening remain in progress.</p></header>
    <section class="product-band" aria-labelledby="product-purpose"><div><div class="eyebrow">Why Ember exists</div><h2 id="product-purpose">A mining engine shouldn't have to be the whole experience.</h2><p>XMRig provides the mining engine. Ember's role is to make setup, consent and local process state easier to follow, while preserving the technical detail when it matters.</p></div><div class="feature-rows"><div class="feature-row"><span>01</span><div><strong>Configure with context</strong><p>Enter a public receiving address and pool details, then review the choices and disclosures before starting.</p></div></div><div class="feature-row"><span>02</span><div><strong>Keep a visible boundary</strong><p>Ember prepares and supervises its own XMRig process; the selected pool remains an external service.</p></div></div><div class="feature-row"><span>03</span><div><strong>Understand the profile</strong><p>Quiet, Balanced and Performance set configured thread counts; they are not wattage or temperature controls.</p></div></div></div></section>
    <section class="section" id="how-it-works" aria-label="How Ember and XMRig relate"><TechnicalFlow /></section>
    <section class="product-band" aria-labelledby="product-controls"><div><div class="eyebrow">What stays visible</div><h2 id="product-controls">Choices before activity. State during a session.</h2><p>The interface is designed to show mining state, supported XMRig telemetry and what Ember has actually observed. Setup is not silently treated as a running session.</p></div><div class="product-status"><article class="status-tile"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> IMPLEMENTED</span><h3>Setup review</h3><p>Wallet, pool, profile and disclosures are reviewed before an explicit start action.</p></article><article class="status-tile"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> IMPLEMENTED</span><h3>Local process control</h3><p>Rust supervises the XMRig child process and its process tree on Windows.</p></article><article class="status-tile is-open"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> STILL VERIFYING</span><h3>Production mining</h3><p>Owner-machine Start → Mining → Stop verification remains unresolved.</p></article><article class="status-tile is-open"><span class="tile-state"><i aria-hidden="true"></i> NOT RELEASED</span><h3>Public distribution</h3><p>Legal review, clean-machine checks and release hardening remain open.</p></article></div></section>
    <section class="section"><div class="cta-panel"><div class="eyebrow">A note on availability</div><h2>Ember is being built, not offered as a public download.</h2><p>Read the implementation and release caveats before forming a view of what the product currently supports.</p><div class="actions"><a class="button button-primary" href="/trust/">Trust and transparency <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></div></section>
  </div>
</SiteLayout>
